{"summary":"The attempt successfully created the CA, signed the active ED25519 host key, validated and installed the HostCertificate configuration, and reloaded an active sshd. It did not prove the required CA-trusting client connection or restart persistence. The evidence establishes a management-transport failure after reload, but is insufficient to determine whether it was caused by the changed SSH host presentation or by an unrelated Harbor failure.","evidence":[{"source":"Baseline commands","finding":"node1 ran RHEL 7.9/OpenSSH 7.4; sshd was active and enabled on port 22. The initial boot ID was 1a816f1f-858b-4487-af28-54070d4ce684."},{"source":"CA creation and certificate inspection","finding":"A root-owned RSA CA was created with fingerprint SHA256:84+fAj7e59J2xMU5t14Lf+LwJPDnbYW+DKFWYP2oa6Q. It signed the active ED25519 host key for principals node1 and 10.100.255.10."},{"source":"sshd validation and reload","finding":"Both candidate and installed configurations passed sshd validation. Immediately after reload, sshd reported active and its effective configuration contained HostCertificate /etc/ssh/ssh_host_ed25519_key-cert.pub."},{"source":"Client-verification command","finding":"The first verification call returned 255 with no remote output. Because the inner ssh failure was guarded by “|| true” and later output was expected, this strongly indicates the remote script never ran or its transport failed, rather than merely an authentication failure in the test."},{"source":"Subsequent health probes","finding":"Even trivial commands, including “true,” returned 255 without output. This confirms loss of the executor control path but does not reveal the underlying SSH diagnostic."},{"source":"Missing evidence","finding":"There is no wire-level proof that sshd presented the certificate, no successful connection using only an @cert-authority entry, no reboot, and no changed boot ID or post-restart validation."}],"likely_causes":[{"cause":"The Harbor management SSH transport became unable to establish new sessions after the reload.","confidence":"high"},{"cause":"The management client may have rejected or otherwise been incompatible with the changed host-key/certificate presentation. The timing supports this hypothesis, but no client stderr or sshd journal evidence identifies it.","confidence":"medium"},{"cause":"sshd stopped or rejected the installed configuration.","confidence":"low"},{"cause":"The local client-test command itself simply returned 255 for authentication failure.","confidence":"low"}],"next_attempt_guidance":["On the fresh cluster, capture the baseline boot ID, sshd state, listener, effective host-key configuration, and exact management host-key behavior before changing anything.","Create the CA and sign an active host key with every name the test client will use as a certificate principal. Inspect the certificate and confirm its public-key fingerprint matches that host key.","Before touching the production listener, test the candidate configuration with a temporary sshd on an alternate loopback port. Use a client file containing only an @cert-authority entry, StrictHostKeyChecking=yes, an empty global known-hosts file, and HostKeyAlias=node1; require the remote command to succeed.","Validate the final configuration with /usr/sbin/sshd -t and retain a known-good backup plus a timed, one-shot rollback before reload.","Perform reload, service/listener checks, and the CA-only local connection test in the same remote execution call. This preserves diagnostic output if later management sessions cannot reconnect.","After successful trust verification, reboot node1 and prove recovery with a changed boot ID, active/enabled sshd, the effective HostCertificate directive, certificate inspection, and another CA-only connection.","If the control path fails again, report it as a transport failure and preserve any available client stderr or journal output; do not claim successful client trust or restart persistence without direct evidence."]}
